+# Content expansion plan
+
+## Constraint and baseline
+
+This is a content-and-page-architecture expansion, not a layout redesign. The existing Mercury site is the only design system: every new page keeps the established banner, five-slot banner navigation, 202px `#col-left` / 764px `#col-right` layout, `Vbox` / `Vnavbox` sidebar panels, `Cbox` content panels, project-card language, and existing footer. Later explicit typography and EQ requests add only narrow CSS refinements that use the existing palette, sprites, spacing language, and panel frames; no alternate visual system or third-party styling is introduced.
+
+## Page map
+
+- `music.html` — the music landing page. The left rail lists recent releases and its player-page links, plus platform links. The content rail contains featured productions, production setup, a customizable 10-band graphic EQ, and tracks with native inline audio controls.
+- `music-player.html?track=<id>` — a dedicated track detail/player page using the same shell. It reads a track from the music catalogue, plays its supplied MP3/audio URL directly, and shares the graphic EQ controls; invalid IDs get an in-site not-found state.
+- `business.html` — company index and profiles. The left rail is a company/year timeline linking to the matching overview panel. Every company profile has its own achievements timeline, description, logo slot, and optional 0–5 success score.
+- `projects.html` — full public repository catalogue for GitLab `paysonism` and GitHub `430L`. The page loads every public repository through each host's public API, filters the combined list by host from the left rail, shows source metadata with icon-led actions, and combines exact normalized name matches (plus a documented CorMem naming alias) into one entry with links to each source.
+
+The current homepage remains the introduction and selected-work teaser. Its banner navigation points to the new catalogue pages. Existing anchor sections and forum access remain reachable, and the forum gets links back into the expanded site.
+
+## Content model and data ownership
+
+No real tracks, production photos, company details, logos, or scores were supplied, so no personal facts are invented. To verify the interfaces, the editable arrays in `js/music-data.js` and `js/business-data.js` now contain clearly labeled dummy records, original generated MP3 EQ-test loops, an illustrative placeholder setup image, sample company logos, and fictional milestone records. Replace or remove these before publishing. A real audio entry supplies a local `assets/music/...` path or public audio URL. Company entries contain an ID, name, start/end years, summary, optional logo, optional 0–5 score, and an ordered list of dated milestones.
+
+Repository data is fetched live rather than copied into a stale hand-maintained snapshot: the APIs provide descriptions, source URLs, stars, forks, default branch, language, dates, archive/fork state, and ZIP download URLs. Both APIs are paginated. If a host is unavailable or rate-limited, the page still provides direct profile links and an explicit load status/error rather than silently presenting an incomplete archive as complete.
+
+## Implementation sequence
+
+1. Keep the existing shell and CSS unchanged; add this plan before page work.
+2. Add repeatable page shells and only existing Mercury classes; add page files to the static build copy list.
+3. Add music/business data contracts and safe DOM-based rendering for galleries, player links, company ratings and per-company milestone lists.
+4. Add the complete, deduplicated two-host project catalogue and status/fallback states.
+5. Connect home/forum navigation to the new pages, then run tests, build, and deploy-file verification.
+
+## Acceptance checks
+
+- New pages reuse the existing CSS file and Mercury panel structure; added typography and EQ styles remain within the current Mercury palette and sprite language.
+- Music page has all requested sections, clearly marked local MP3 test tracks, recent-project sidebar links, and a working EQ on each player. EQ uses smooth native peaking biquads, a frequency-response-derived headroom trim, a gentle final safety compressor, presets, bypass, and persisted custom band/preamp values.
+- Dedicated player resolves an ID and has a direct audio source; missing content is clearly represented without fabricated tracks.
+- Business sidebar years link to company panels, each profile owns its own milestone list, and unknown values are not fabricated.
+- Projects fetch all pages from both public APIs, preserve one combined entry for duplicates while retaining both source/download links, include current stars and other available metadata, and source filters work without navigation.
+- `npm run verify` passes and the new pages are included in `dist/client/`.
